    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1006-a1">

                <pb facs="0971" n="713" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg713"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grey</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the American
                        Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 29147/15]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p>The Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs presents his compliments to the United
                    States Ambassador, and has the honour to inform him that he forwarded to the
                    Department of His Majesty’s Government concerned, his excellency’s note of the
                    3d instant transmitting copy of a letter from the Western Union Telegraph
                    Company.</p>
                <p>The censorship authorities point out, in reply, that so far-reaching a question
                    as the admission or prohibition of code to neutral countries in Europe is one
                    that must be decided without reference to the views that may be held by a
                    private cable company, and so far as they are aware, nothing has occurred to
                    modify the necessity of retaining the power of complete control over trade
                    telegrams to or from neutral countries adjacent to Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-18">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 18, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
